  <subsection xml:id="changing-lists">
    <title>Changing List Elements with Assignment</title>
  <idx><h>list</h><h>assignment</h></idx>



    <p>
      Assignment (<c>=</c>) can be used to change the value of an element in a list. You can use an indexed
      list variable like <c>myList[index] = value</c> on the left side of the assignment operator just like a regular variable. 
      For example, the code below changes the value in <c>scores[0]</c> to 100 using assignment:
    </p>

    <program language="python">
        <code>
# A list of scores
scores = [95, 87, 92, 100]
# Change the first score to 100
scores[0] = 100
          </code> 
      </program>

    <activity label="fruit-list-change">
    <statement><p>Change the <c>fruits</c> list below by changing apple to mango using assignment.</p></statement>
     <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]
# TODO: fill in the index number inside the [ ] to print out "banana"
print( fruits[   ] )
# TODO: Fill in the index number inside the [ ] to change "apple" to "mango"
fruits[   ] = "mango"
# TODO: Use assignment to change cherry in the fruits list to your favorite fruit

print(fruits)
        </code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertIn("fruits[1]", self.getEditorText(), "Use fruits[1]")
        self.assertIn('fruits[0]="mango"', self.getEditorText().replace(' ', ''), "Set fruits[0] to \"mango\"")
        self.assertIn("[\'mango\', \'banana\',", self.getOutput(), "Output for fruits list")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
        </program>
</activity>


    <p>Click on next in the following Code Lens to see how the list is created
        in memory and then changed with assignment statements.
    </p>
   <activity label="list-codelens">
     <statement>
        <p>
          Click on the Next button at the bottom of the code to see how
          the values in the list  change as you step through the running
          program. You can see interactive tracing in any Active Code exercise by 
          clicking the Code Lens button. 
        </p>
      </statement>
     <program interactive="codelens" language="python">
    <code>
numbers = [1,2,3,4]
numbers[0] = 2
numbers[1] = numbers[0] + 2
numbers[2] *= 2 
numbers[3] = numbers[3] + numbers[1]
print(numbers)
    </code>
  </program>
   </activity>
  
    <activity label="list-trace1">
    <statement>
      <p> What is printed by the following statements? See the Code Lens above.</p>
       <program  language="python">
    <code>
numbers = [1,2]
numbers[0] = 2
numbers[1] = numbers[0] + 2
print(numbers)
    </code>
    </program>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[1, 2]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No, the list is changed.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[2, 4, 1, 2]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No, assignment changes the values. It does not append to the list.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>[2, 4]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Yes, see how each line is calculated in the Code Lens above.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[2, 3]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No, the 0th element is 2 when the third line is executed.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <activity label="list-trace2">
    <statement>
      <p> What would the following code print?</p>
<program language="python">
    <code>
values = [3, 2, 1]
values[0] = values[1]
values[2] = values[2] + 1
print(values)
    </code>
    </program>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[3, 2, 1]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>That is the original contents of <code>values</code>, but the contents are changed.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[2, 0, 2]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No, when you set <code>values[0]</code> to <code>values[1]</code> it makes a copy of the value and doesn't zero it out.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>[2, 2, 2]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Yes, the value at index 0 is set to a copy of the value at index 1 and the value at index 2 is incremented.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[2, 3, 1]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No, the element at index 2 is the last element in the list since we start counting at 0.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>



    <p>Before making changes to a list, programmers sometimes make a copy of it first using assignment. 
        Then the original list can be preserved and used later if needed. 
    </p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
# A list of scores
scores = [95, 87, 92, 100]
# Make a copy of the scores list
scores_bonus = scores
# Add bonus points to the first two scores in the copy
scores_bonus[0] += 5
scores_bonus[1] += 5
</code>
</program>
</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="append">
    <title>Append and Length of List</title>
 <idx>append</idx>
 <idx>empty list</idx>
 <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>empty</h>
    </idx>
 <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>append</h>
    </idx>



    <p>If you do not know what values you want to store in a list when you create it, 
        you can start with <term>empty list ([])</term> which is a list that contains no elements and
        add in elements while the program is running. For example, we can start with an empty shopping list.</p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
# An empty shopping list
shopping_list = []
        </code>
</program>


    <p>Elements can be added to a list using the <c>append</c> function which adds the element 
        to the end of the list.</p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
# An empty shopping list
shopping_list = []
shopping_list.append("milk")
shopping_list.append("eggs")
shopping_list.append("bread")
print(shopping_list)
        </code>
        </program>

         <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>length</h>
    </idx>
    <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>len</h>
    </idx>

      
        
    <p>
    In Python, you can get the length of a list by using the <c>len(aList)</c> function, note the
    shortened form of len for length. In AP Pseudocode, the function used is <c>length(aList)</c>.
    </p>

    <program language="python">
        <code>  
# A list of names
names = ["Jordan", "Alex", "Sam", "Taylor"]
# Get the length of the names list
print("The length of the names list is:", len(names))
        </code>
    </program>

      <p>The length of the list will increase as elements are added. 
    Let's try <c>append</c> and <c>len</c> below.</p>

    <activity label="append-to-list">
    <statement><p>Add two more items to the <c>shopping_list</c> below. Then, print the list and the length of the list.
    </p></statement>
        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>
shopping_list = []
shopping_list.append("milk")
shopping_list.append("eggs")
shopping_list.append("bread")
print("Shopping list: ", shopping_list)
print("Length of shopping list: ", len(shopping_list))

# Add 2 more elements to the shopping_list  
# Then print the list and its length!

</code>
            <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertLessEqual(5,len(shopping_list), "shopping_list should have at least 5 elements")
        count = self.getEditorText().count("append")
        self.assertLessEqual(5,count, "at least 5 appends used")
        count = self.getEditorText().count("len(")
        self.assertLessEqual(2,count, "at least 2 len() calls used")

myTests().main()
            </tests>
        </program>
    </activity>



  </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="insert-remove-lists">
    <title>Insert and Remove List Elements</title>

        <idx>insert</idx>
         <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>insert</h>
    </idx>
     <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>remove</h>
    </idx>



    <p>Python also provides built-in functions to change list elements. We already saw the <c>append()</c> 
      function, which adds an element to the end of a list. We can also use 
      <c>list.insert(index, element)</c> that adds an element at a 
       specific index and moves the elements after it one position to the right. It's important to remember that
    append, insert, and remove always change the length of the list moving elements to the right or left as needed!</p>

    <p>In the AP CSP Pseudocode, the functions to change a list are similar to those in Python, but the AP CSP pseudocode
        <c>list.remove(index)</c> function removes the element from the list at a certain index. 
        Python  has  <c>list.remove(value)</c> which deletes the very first occurrence 
       of a specific value from the list or <c>list.pop(index)</c> which removes and returns 
       the element at a specific index, but not a <c>list.remove(index)</c> function.
    </p>


    <activity label="insert-remove-puppies">
    <statement>
        <p>The code below uses a list to keep track of dogs at the animal shelter.
            Try the insert and remove functions in the code below. Then follow the directions in the comments
            to insert and remove elements from the list.</p>
    </statement>
        
        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>
# List of puppies in alphabetical order at the shelter
puppies = ["Biscuit", "Coco", "Daisy"]
print("Puppies at the shelter:", puppies)

# .append() adds a new puppy to the end of the list
puppies.append("Mochi")
print("A new puppy arrived! Updated list:", puppies)

# .remove() removes a puppy by its name when it gets adopted
puppies.remove("Coco")
print("Coco got adopted! Remaining puppies:", puppies)

# Use insert to add "Buddy" at the correct index to keep the list in alphabetical order

# Use remove to remove "Daisy" from the list since she got adopted

# Use insert or append to add another puppy

# Print the remaining puppies
            </code>
            <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
        self.assertIn('puppies.insert(1,"Buddy")', code, "Use insert to add Buddy at correct index")
        self.assertIn('puppies.remove("Daisy")', code, "Use remove to remove Daisy")

myTests().main()
            </tests>
            </program>
        </activity>
        
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="tracing-lists-errors">
    <title>Tracing List Operations and Index Errors</title>

            <idx><h>unplugged</h><h>lists</h></idx>
    <idx><h>projects</h><h>unplugged list of chairs 2</h></idx>

        <project label="unplugged-list-of-chairs-revisited">
       <title>Unplugged List of Chairs Revisited</title>
      <statement>
    <p>
      Let's revisit the unplugged activity with students in chairs and simulate append, insert, and remove operations.
        In groups or as a class or individually, organize a line of empty chairs as the list. Have some extra chairs ready to append or insert.
        Each student who sits in a chair represents an element in the list.
        The first chair in the line has the index 0. The second chair has the index 1, and so on.
        Have students follow the code below to insert and remove students and chairs.
        Substitute in the names of students in your class.
    </p>
        <program language="python">
            <code>
# Fill the chairs with students
chairs = ["Alex", "Sam", "Ryan"]
# Example code to insert and remove students in chairs
chairs.append("Dani") # Add a new chair and student to the end of the list
# Remove the student in chair 1, move students left 1 chair, 
#   and remove the last chair
chairs.remove(1) 
# Insert a new student in chair 1 and move students to the right to make room.
chairs.insert(1, "Taylor") 
            </code>
        </program>
       <p>    
       How do append, insert, and remove affect the list differently than changing the values of existing elements? 
       Write your own code below using append, insert, and remove, and then act it out.         
      </p></statement>

       <response/>
     </project>

    

     <activity label="list-trace-insert-remove">
    <statement>
      <p> 
What are the values in the list after executing the following AP Pseudocode? Remember that list indexing starts at 0
and the pseudocode uses <c>list.insert(index, value)</c> and <c>list.remove(index)</c> to change the list. Insert and
remove always change the length of the list moving elements to the right or left as needed.
To trace this code, write down the list after each line of code.
      </p>
      <program language="python">
        <code>
list = [ 0, 3, 5 ]
list.append(7)
list.insert(0, 1)
list.remove(1)
        </code>
      </program>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>[1, 3, 5, 7]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Yes, the list is updated as follows: [0, 3, 5], after appends [0, 3, 5, 7], then insert [1, 0, 3, 5, 7], then remove [1, 3, 5, 7].</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[0, 1, 3, 5, 7]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Don't forget the remove!</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[0, 3, 5, 7]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No, this is correct after the append, but not after the insert.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>[3, 5, 7]</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>No, the insert does not replace the element but inserts and moves everything down.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
    </activity>
  


    <p>What happens if you try to access an element that is not there? 
        Try to access puppies at index 7 below to see what happens. 
        The index must be between 0 and the length of the list - 1 or it will give an error message 
        "IndexError: list index out of range".</p>
   
  <activity label="index-out-of-range">
    <statement>
        <p>Run the code below to see an index out of range error. Fix the error by changing the index. </p>
            </statement>
        
        <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
            <code>
# List of puppies in alphabetical order at the shelter
puppies = ["Biscuit", "Coco", "Daisy"]
print("Puppies at the shelter:", puppies)

# Try to access the last puppy - fix the error
print("The last puppy on the list is:", puppies[3])  
            </code>
            <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertIn("puppies[2]", self.getEditorText(), "Use puppies[2] to access the last puppy")

myTests().main()
            </tests>
            </program>
        </activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="random-slicing">
    <title>Random Elements and List Slicing</title>
   
    <idx>random index</idx>
     <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>random index</h>
    </idx>
    



<p>Sometimes you may want to pick a random element from the list.  One way to do this is to generate a random index. It would
    start at 0 and go up to the length of the list minus 1. If there are 3 elements in the list, the valid indices would be 0, 1, and 2. 
    In Python, you can use the <c>random.randint()</c> function to generate a random index. 
    The valid indices are 0 through <c>len(list) - 1</c>. 
    For example, if you have a list of songs, you can select a random song from the playlist using the code <c>random.randint(0, len(songs) - 1)</c>.
    Python actually has a built-in function called <c>random.choice(list)</c> that will select a random element from a list, 
    but we will use the random index method here to match AP pseudocode.
    </p>
    <program language="python">
        <code>
import random
songs = ["Song 1", "Song 2", "Song 3", "Song 4"]
random_index = random.randint(0, len(songs) - 1)
random_song = songs[random_index]
print(random_song)
        </code>
      </program>
    
    <activity label="random-emoji">
    <statement>
      <p>Use the code below to select a random emoji from the list. 
        Use the <c>random.randint()</c> function to generate a random index and then use that index to access an element in the list.
        Run the code multiple times to see different emojis printed!
      </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
import random
emojis = ["😀", "😂", "😍", "🤔", "😎"]
# create a random index from 0 to the length of the list minus 1

# Use the random index to print a random emoji from the list

</code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
            code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
            self.assertIn("random.randint(0,len(emojis)-1)", code, "Use random.randint from 0 to len(emojis)-1")
            self.assertIn("emojis[random_index]", code, "Use the random index to access an element in the list")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
        </program>
    </activity>

     <idx>
      <h>list</h><h>slicing</h>
    </idx>
    <idx>slicing</idx>

<p>Python also has a cool slicing operator (<c>[start:end]</c>) that allows you to extract a portion of a list from 
    a start index up to (but not including) an end index, 
    just like we saw with strings.
</p>

  <activity label="list-slice">
    <statement>
      <p> Write code that prints out slices of the emoji list, using [start-index : up-to-end-index]. 
      </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
emojis = ["😀", "😂", "😍", "🤔", "😎"]
# Print the last 3 emojis using slicing.
print("Last 3 emojis:", emojis[2:5])

# print the first 3 emojis using slicing

</code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
            self.assertIn("emojis[0:3]", self.getEditorText(), "Use slicing [0:3] to print the first 3 emojis")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
        </program>
    </activity>

</subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="list-loops">
    <title>Traversing Lists with Loops</title>
<idx><h>list</h><h>traversal</h></idx>


    <p>Python has a simple way to <term>traverse</term> (step through) a list, visiting each element in the list, using a for loop with 
      the pattern: <c>for item in list:</c> 
   </p>

   <program language="python">
      <code>
aList = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
for item in aList:
    print(item)
      </code>
    </program>

     <activity label="loop-emojis">
    <statement>
      <p> Write code that uses a for loop to print out each emoji in the list on a separate line.
      </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
puppies = ["Biscuit", "Coco", "Daisy"]
# A for-loop can print each puppy in the list on a separate line
for puppy in puppies:
    print(puppy)

emojis = ["😀", "😂", "😍", "🤔", "😎"]
# Use a for-loop to print each emoji on a separate line

</code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertRegex(self.getEditorText(), r"for \w+ in emojis:", "Use a for loop to traverse the list of emojis")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
        </program>
    </activity>

    <p>An index-controlled for-loop using <c>range(len(aList))</c> or a while loop can also be used 
      to traverse a list. The loop variable is the index to access each element in the list. This 
      is why programmers often use the variable i, short for index, as the loop variable. However, 
      it is a lot more complex. If you do not need the index, it is simpler to use the <c>for item 
      in list</c> loop instead. The code below compares all three loops. The first one is the simplest and most often used with lists. </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
aList = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
# Simple for loop for lists
for item in aList:
    print(item)

# More complex indexed for-loop
for i in range(len(aList)):
    print( aList[i] )

# More complex while loop
i = 0
while i &lt; len(aList):
    print( aList[i] )
    i += 1
        </code>
        </program>

          <activity label="list-indexed-loop">
    <statement>
      <p>Write code that uses an indexed for loop using <c>range(0,len(list))</c> to print out each emoji in the list 
        on a separate line with the index.
      </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
puppies = ["Biscuit", "Coco", "Daisy"]
# A for-loop can print each puppy in the list on a separate line
for puppy in puppies:
    print(puppy)
# An indexed for-loop can print each puppy in the list on a separate line with the index
for i in range(len(puppies)):
    print(i, puppies[i])

emojis = ["😀", "😂", "😍", "🤔", "😎"]
# Change the for-loop to an indexed for-loop to print each emoji on a separate line with its index
for item in emojis:
    print(item)

</code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        self.assertRegex(self.getEditorText(), r"for \w+ in range\(len\(emojis\)\):", "Use an indexed for loop to traverse the list of emojis")
        self.assertIn("range(len(emojis))", self.getEditorText(), "Use range(len(emojis)) to traverse the list of emojis")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
        </program>
    </activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="list-accumulator">
    <title>Accumulator Pattern with Lists</title>
    

    <idx><h>list</h><h>accumulator pattern</h></idx>

    <p>When we were learning about loops, we learned about the <term>accumulator pattern</term>
      which uses a loop and an <term>accumulator variable</term> to keep track of the running total 
      as each number is added in.  
      This accumulator pattern is often used to process lists and perform calculations on the elements.
 </p>
 
   <p>The Accumulator Pattern or Algorithm has the following steps:</p>
   <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Initialize the accumulator variable before the loop.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Loop through the values.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Update the accumulator variable inside the loop.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Print or use the accumulated value when the loop is done.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

      <p>
      For example, the <c>count</c> variable can be an accumulator variable to count the number of elements in a list 
      or <c>sum</c> can be an accumulator variable to calculate the sum of all the elements in a list. We can compute the average of
      a list by dividing the sum by the length of the list using <c>len(list)</c>.
    </p>
   

    <program language="python">
      <code>
scores = [ 100, 90, 95, 85, 93 ]
sum = 0

for number in scores:
    sum += number
      </code>
    </program>

     <activity label="mixedup-average-list" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following program has the correct code to return the average of
          a list of numbers, but the code is mixed up. Drag the
          blocks from the left into the correct order on the right. You will be
          told if any of the blocks are in the wrong order or are indented
          incorrectly.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="4">
          <cline>sum = 0</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="2">
          <cline>for number in numbers:</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="3">
          <cline>    sum += number</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="5">
          <cline>print("Average:", sum/len(numbers))</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>

    <activity label="list-count">
      <statement>
        <p>Count the number of elements in the list using a for loop and an accumulator variable.</p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
aList = [0, 10.5, 3, 7, 2.5, 8, 4, 1]
count = 0
# Use a for-loop to traverse the list, print each element, and increment the count
   

print("The number of elements in the list is:", count)
        </code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
  def test1(self):
            self.assertIn("for ", self.getEditorText(), "Use a for loop to traverse the list")
            self.assertIn("count+=1", self.getEditorText().replace(" ", ""), "Increment the count for each element in the list")
            self.assertIn("8", self.getOutput(), "The count should be 8")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </activity>

     <activity label="list-accumulator-practice">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the accumulator pattern to add taxes to each price in a list of prices. Try Code Lens to see the variable values change as the program runs. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
prices = [2.99, 5.49, 3.99, 4.99]
total = 0
tax_rate = 0.07

# Use a for-loop to go through prices
#     compute the tax for each price (the price times the tax rate)
#     add the price and its tax to the total


print("Total", total)
        </code>      
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
  def test1(self):
      code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
      self.assertIn("for ", self.getEditorText(), "Use a for loop to traverse the list")
      self.assertIn("total+=", code, "Use += to add the price and tax to the total")
      self.assertIn("*", code, "Multiply the price by the tax rate")
      self.assertEqual(18.6822, total, "The total should be 18.6822")

myTests().main()
        </tests>
        </program>
      </activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="coding-challenge-lists1">
    <title>Coding Challenge: Parallel Lists in a Bakery</title>
       <idx><h>projects</h><h>lists bakery</h></idx>
      <idx><h>coding challenge</h><h>lists bakery</h></idx>

      <p>A bakery uses parallel lists to store the names, emoji images, and prices of baked goods 
        in three lists. The lists are kept in parallel order so that an index can be used to access 
        the corresponding elements in each list. For example, the first element in the list of 
        baked goods corresponds to the first element in the list of prices, the second element 
        corresponds to the second element, and so on. This allows a program to easily look up the 
        price and image of a baked good by index. In the following coding challenge, you will use 
        an input-controlled while loop to  ask the user to choose baked goods from the bakery and 
        put its index in an order list. You will then print out their receipt and calculate the 
        total cost of their order using a for loop and the order list.
      </p>

    <project label="coding-challenge-parallel-lists">
      <title>Bakery Parallel Lists</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A bakery uses 3 parallel lists below to store the names, emoji images, and prices of baked goods. The while-loop below prints the lists and asks for the user's order by choosing an index number. You will write code to append the chosen index to the order list after checking that it is in range. You will then print out their receipt and calculate the total cost of their order using a for loop and the order list.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
# Bakery parallel lists
baked_goods = ["Donut", "Cookie", "Croissant", "Cupcake", "Bagel" ]
images = ["🍩", "🍪", "🥐", "🧁", "🥯", ]
prices = [1.50, 2.00, 3.00, 2.50, 1.75]

print("Welcome to the Bakery! Here are our baked goods:")
print(images)
print(baked_goods)
print(prices)

# Empty Order list of indices of baked goods chosen by the user
order = []
choice = 0
while choice != -1:
    choice = int(input("Enter the index of the baked good you want to order (or -1 to end): "))
    # TODO: Check if the choice is a valid index between 0 and the length of baked_goods - 1
    #      If so, append choice to the order list
    # else print an error message and ask the user to try again 


# TODO: Print the receipt and calculate the total cost of the order
# using a for-loop through the order list and the indices to access the baked goods and prices lists

</code>
<tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
            code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
            self.assertIn("if choice", self.getEditorText(), "Use an if-statement to check if the choice is a valid index")
            self.assertIn("len(", self.getEditorText(), "Use len() to check the length of a list in the if-statement")
            self.assertIn("else:", self.getEditorText(), "Use an else-statement to handle invalid input")
            self.assertIn("order.append(choice)", self.getEditorText(), "Append the choice to the order list")
            self.assertIn("for ", self.getEditorText(), "Use a for loop to traverse the order list")
            self.assertIn("baked_goods[", code, "Access the name of each baked good using its index")
            self.assertIn("prices[", code, "Access the price of each baked good using its index")
            self.assertIn("+=", code, "Use += to add the price to the total cost")


myTests().main()
</tests>
</program>
</project>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="linear-search">
    <title>Linear Search</title>
<idx>linear search</idx>
<idx>sequential search</idx>
<idx>search</idx>
  <idx><h>list</h><h>search</h></idx>

    <p>Another common algorithm with lists is searching for a particular item in the list. 
      <term>Linear Search</term> (also called <term>sequential search</term>) is a simple search 
      algorithm that checks each element in the list one by one until it finds the desired value or 
      reaches the end of the list. Watch the animation of the algorithm below of searching for the value 33 in a list of numbers.
    </p>

    <image source="_static/Unit3images/linearsearch.gif" alt="Linear Search" width="70%"/>



    <p>The searching pattern has an if-statement inside the loop to check if you have found the correct value.</p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
search_value = int(input("Enter a number to search for: "))
for number in numbers:
    if number == search_value:
        print("Found!")
      </code>
</program>



<p>If you are only looking for the first occurrence of a value, you can use a <c>break</c> 
  statement on a line on its own to exit the loop once the value is found. This is called a 
  <term>partial traversal</term> where only some elements of the list are visited. 
  The <c>break</c> statement will immediately exit the 
  loop. A boolean flag variable can be used to indicate whether the value was found. Try it out 
  below.</p>

    <activity label="mixedup-linear-search" numbered="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following program has the correct code for linear search, but the code is mixed up. Drag the
          blocks from the left into the correct order on the right. You will be
          told if any of the blocks are in the wrong order or are indented
          incorrectly.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]</cline>
          <cline>found = False</cline>
          <cline>search_value = 100</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="2">
          <cline>for number in numbers:</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="3">
          <cline>    if number == search_value:</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="5">
          <cline>        found = True</cline>
          <cline>        break</cline>
        </block>
        <block order="4">
          <cline>if found:</cline>
          </block>
        <block order="6">
          <cline>    print("Found!")</cline>
        </block>
        <block>
          <cline>else:</cline>
          </block>
          <block>
          <cline>    print("Not found.")</cline>
          </block>
      </blocks>
    </activity>

<activity label="linear-search-with-break">
      <statement>
        <p>Complete the following linear search algorithm. This algorithm uses a boolean flag to indicate whether the value was found and allows for early termination of the loop with break.</p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
search_value = int(input("Enter a number to search for: "))
found = False
# Use a for-loop to traverse the list
#     Use an if-statement to check if the number is equal to the search_value
#     If it is, set found to True and break out of the loop


# After the loop
if found:
    print("Found!")
else:
    print("Not found.")
        </code>      
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
            code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
            self.assertIn("for ", self.getEditorText(), "Use a for loop to traverse the list")
            self.assertIn("   if", self.getEditorText(), "Use an if statement inside the loop")
            self.assertIn("found=True", code, "Set found to True inside if")
            self.assertIn("   break", self.getEditorText(), "Use break if the number is found")

myTests().main()
          </tests>
        </program>
      </activity>



  <idx>minimum</idx>
  <idx>maximum</idx>
  <idx><h>list</h><h>minimum</h></idx>
  <idx><h>list</h><h>maximum</h></idx>

    <p>The find minimum and maximum algorithms that we looked at with loops are just a variation
      the search pattern. They also have an if-statement inside the loop to check if the current element is less than or greater than the current minimum or maximum.
    </p>

    <activity label="max-list">
      <statement><p>Run the following code to see the minimum algorithm in action. Then, change the code to find the maximum value instead of the minimum.
      </p></statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>
numbersList = [90, 50, 75, 25, 100]
# Assume the first number is the minimum
min = numbersList[0]
for num in numbersList:
    if num &lt; min:
        min = num

print("The minimum value in the list is", min)
      </code>
      <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
   def test1(self):
       self.assertIn("max",self.getEditorText(),"Use the variable max")
       self.assertIn("&gt;",self.getEditorText(), "Use &gt; to find the maximum")
       self.assertIn("100", self.getOutput(), "The maximum value should be 100")

  
myTests().main()
      </tests>
    </program>
   </activity>    

  <idx><h>list</h><h>tracing</h></idx>


    <p>When tracing through code with lists, it helps to draw the list and its elements on paper 
      or a whiteboard to visualize the process. The Code Lens button on the activecode activities 
      or <url href="https://pythontutor.com/visualize.html#mode=edit">Python Tutor</url> can also help you visualize the 
      execution of your code step by step.</p>

<activity label="list-loop-tracing">
  <statement>
    <p>Trace the following code to determine count at the end of the loop. If you get stuck, try it
      in an activecode window (or the pencil icon scratch code up at the top) and use Code Lens.
    </p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
count = 0
for number in numbers:
    if number % 2 == 0:
        count += 1
      </code>
    </program>
    <p>The value of <c>count</c> after the loop: <fillin answer="3" mode="string" /></p>
  </statement>
<evaluation>
    <evaluate>
      <test>
        <strcmp use-answer="yes" />
        <feedback>
          Correct! This loop counts the number of even numbers in the list. The even numbers are 2, 4, and 6, so the count is 3. 
        </feedback>
      </test>

      <test>
        <!-- else -->
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Remember that <c>number % 2 == 0</c> checks if a number is evenly divided by 2 with no remainder. This means it is an even number. 
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </test>
    </evaluate>
  </evaluation>
</activity>

<activity label="list-csp-pseudocode">
  <statement>
    <p>The following pseudocode is intended to return the number of times the value
<c>val</c> appears in the list <c>myList</c>. It does not work as intended.</p>
<pre>
Line 1: for item in myList
Line 2: {
Line 3:     count = 0
Line 4:     if (item == val)
Line 5:     {
Line 6:         count = count + 1
Line 7:     }
Line 8: }
</pre>
<p>
Which of the following changes can be made so that the code will work
as intended?
</p>
</statement>
  <choices randomize="yes">
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>Moving the statement in line 3 so that it appears before line 1</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Correct! The count variable should be initialized to 0 before the loop starts, not inside the loop. Otherwise, it will reset to 0 on each iteration.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>Changing line 4 to <c>if (item == count)</c></p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>No, we need to use the <c>==</c> operator to test, not the assignment (<c>=</c>) operator.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>Changing line 4 to <c>if (myList[item] == val)</c></p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>No, item is an element from the list, not an index.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>Moving the statement in line 6 so that it appears after line 7</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>No, this change would not fix the issue of resetting count on each iteration.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</activity>

<activity label="list-csp-pseudocode2">
  <statement>
    <p>Which code below could be placed in the following loop to print out the item in a list that 
      has the lowest (minimum) value?</p>
    <pre>
list = [1, 0, 4, 2]
x = 99
for item in myList
{
    &lt;MISSING CODE&gt;
}
print(x)
    </pre>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <pre>
if (item &gt; 99)
    x = item
       </pre>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>No. This condition looks for an item greater than 99, but none of the items in the list meet that condition.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <pre>
if (item &lt; 99)
   x = item
  </pre>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Not quite. This would assign every item that is less than 99 to <c>x</c>, so the final value would be 2 rather than the minimum value, 0.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <pre>
if (item &gt; x)
    x = item
  </pre>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>No. This condition would replace <c>x</c> when an item is greater than the current value of <c>x</c>, which would find a maximum rather than a minimum.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <pre>
if (item &lt; x)
    x = item
    </pre>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Correct! Starting with <c>x</c> equal to 99, the code replaces <c>x</c> whenever it finds a smaller item. After checking every item, <c>x</c> contains the minimum value, 0.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="data-abstraction-with-lists">
    <title>Data Abstraction with Lists</title>

    <p>In previous lessons, we talked about <term>abstraction</term> in computer science which is 
      about focusing on the general features while hiding the specific details.  
    <term>Data Abstraction</term> is the process of hiding the complex implementation details of a 
    data structure and showing only the needed features to the user. Variables are a type of data 
    abstraction because they allow programmers to use a name to represent a value without needing 
    to know how the value is stored in memory.  Lists are another type of data abstraction because 
    they allow programmers to use a single variable name to represent a collection of values 
    without needing to know how the values are stored or organized in memory. Data abstraction 
    using lists <term>reduces complexity</term> in code and makes it easier to read, write, and maintain.
    </p>


  <activity label="list-data-abstraction">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A programmer creates a program that displays the names of all students
        enrolled in a class. Which implementation best demonstrates the use of
        data abstraction?
      </p>
    </statement>
<choices randomize="yes">
  <choice>
    <statement>
      
      <program language="python">
        <code>
  student1 = "Alex"
  student2 = "Marion"
  student3 = "Jordan"
        </code>
      </program>
          
    </statement>

    <feedback>
      <p>
        No. This implementation uses a separate variable for each student
        rather than treating the related values as a single collection.
      </p>
    </feedback>
  </choice>

  <choice correct="yes">
    <statement>
     
      <program language="python">
        <code>
students = ["Alex", "Marion", "Jordan"]
        </code>
      </program>
    </statement>

    <feedback>
      <p>
        Yes. A list allows related values to be treated as a single data
        abstraction. The programmer can use the name <code>students</code>
        to work with the collection without needing separate variables
        for each student.
      </p>
    </feedback>
  </choice>

  <choice>
    <statement>

      <program language="python">
        <code>
  studentCount = 3
        </code>
      </program>
    </statement>

    <feedback>
      <p>
        No. This variable stores the number of students but does not provide
        a collection containing the students' names.
      </p>
    </feedback>
  </choice>

  <choice>
    <statement>
      <program language="python">
        <code>
print("Alex")
print("Marion")
print("Jordan")
        </code>
      </program>
    </statement>

    <feedback>
      <p>
        No. This implementation contains repeated statements instead of
        using a single abstraction to represent the related student names.
      </p>
    </feedback>
  </choice>
</choices>
</activity>
    

    <p>Data abstraction also makes it easier to add data without having to rewrite the algorithms. 
      Try adding data to the list below. You will see that you do not need to rewrite the loop 
      processing the list at all. This makes it much easier to maintain and update the code.</p>

      <activity label="add-data-to-list">
    <statement><p>Add at least 3 more colors to the list below. Do you need to change the for-loop? 
    </p></statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>  
# Add at least 3 more colors to the list
colors = ["Red", "Green", "Blue"]

# Run to see we do not need to change the loop!
for color in colors:
    print(color)
      </code>
      <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
          self.assertLessEqual(6, len(colors), "Add at least 3 more colors to the list")

myTests().main()
      </tests>
    </program>
  </activity>



    <p>Data abstraction using lists also removes a lot of repetitive duplication in code. Instead of having 
      multiple variables and repeated code to process each variable, we can use a single list and a loop to process all the elements.
    </p>
  
<p><term>Refactoring code</term> means to rewrite the code to improve it while maintaining the 
same functionality. Adding data abstraction is a great way to refactor code to reduce its 
complexity and make it more maintainable and easier to understand. Try it below! </p>

  <activity label="rewrite-as-list">
    <statement><p>Refactor the following code to use a list instead of individual variables.
      Make sure you use a for-loop to traverse the list and print each favorite food.
     
    </p></statement>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <code>  
# Refactor to use a list instead of individual variables
favoriteFood1 = "Pizza"
favoriteFood2 = "Hamburger"
favoriteFood3 = "Sushi"

# Refactor to use a for-loop
print("My favorite foods are:")
print(favoriteFood1)
print(favoriteFood2)
print(favoriteFood3)
      </code>
      <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
          self.assertIn("[", self.getEditorText(), "Use a list to store the favorite foods")
          self.assertIn("for ", self.getEditorText(), "Use a for-loop to print the list")
           
myTests().main()
      </tests>
    </program>
  </activity>

<activity label="reduce-complexity">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the following code.
    </p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
temperatures = [72, 75, 68, 70]
total = 0
for temperature in temperatures:
    total = total + temperature
print(total)</code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Which of the following are reasons why using data abstraction with a list reduces program
      complexity? Select two answers.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The same algorithm can process the data even if we add new temperatures or update or  remove temperatures.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          Yes. The loop processes each element in the list, so the same
          algorithm can work with different amounts of data without needing
          to be rewritten.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The list reduces the need for repeated variables and statements
          for processing individual temperatures.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          Yes. Without a list, the programmer might need separate variables
          and repeated statements for each temperature. Using a list and loop
          reduces this repeated code.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The list automatically calculates the sum of the temperatures.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          No. The list only stores the temperatures. The <code>for</code> loop
          and addition operation are responsible for calculating the sum.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The list guarantees that there are no duplicate temperatures. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          No. A list can contain duplicate values. Preventing duplicate values
          is not a reason why using a list reduces program complexity.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</activity>


  </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="appinventor-quiz-app-link">
      <title>App Inventor Quiz App Project (Optional)</title>

      <p>If your class is using MIT's App Inventor, you may want to do the optional 
   <url href="appinventor-quiz-app.html">App Inventor Quiz App Lesson (sections 3.8.1-3.8.3)</url>
     before you try it in the Python coding challenge below. You can finish sections 3.8.4-3.8.5
     which use a search procedure in the next lesson on procedures.
    </p>
      
      </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="coding-challenge-lists2">
    <title>Coding Challenge: Quiz App</title>
       <idx><h>projects</h><h>lists quiz app</h></idx>
      <idx><h>coding challenge</h><h>lists quiz app</h></idx>

      <p>In this challenge, you will use parallel lists that contain data about the Computer Science 
        Pioneers shown below. You will create a search app and a
        quiz app. There are 3 parts to this challenge: the search app, the quiz app, and 
        the create your own data and app.
      </p>

    <datafile label="AdaLovelace" xml:id="AdaLovelace" filename="AdaLovelace.png" hide="yes">
        <image source="_static/Unit3images/AdaLovelace.png" width="50%" hide="yes"/>
    </datafile>
    <datafile label="AlanTuring" xml:id="AlanTuring" filename="AlanTuring.png" hide="yes">
        <image source="_static/Unit3images/AlanTuring.png" width="50%"/>
    </datafile>
    <datafile label="GraceHopper" xml:id="GraceHopper" filename="GraceHopper.png" hide="yes">
        <image source="_static/Unit3images/GraceHopper.png" width="50%"/>
    </datafile>
    <datafile label="MaryJackson" xml:id="MaryJackson" filename="MaryJackson.png" hide="yes">
        <image source="_static/Unit3images/MaryJackson.png" width="50%"/>
    </datafile>
      
      
      <p>The first part of this challenge is to use a search algorithm to find and display information from the lists.</p>

 <project label="coding-challenge-search">
      <title>Search App</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The search app allows users to search for information in the 3 parallel lists that store the names of pioneers, 
          facts about their contributions, and their images. A special function called draw is provided to display the image of the 
          pioneer. First, present the list of pioneers using a simple <c>for item in list</c> loop. Ask the user who they want to learn 
          about.
          Then, use a for-loop with <c>range(len(pioneers))</c> to search through the pioneers list and check if the user's input 
          is in the list. If it is, print the corresponding fact and display the image of the pioneer by using <c>draw(images[i])</c> 
          where i is the index of the pioneer in the pioneers list. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python" add-files="AdaLovelace,AlanTuring,GraceHopper,MaryJackson">
          <preamble visible="no">
# Do not change the following draw function. It will display the image of the pioneer.
from image import *
def draw(image):
    img = Image(image)
    win = ImageWin(img.getWidth(), img.getHeight())
    img.draw(win)
          </preamble>
        <code>
# Search App
# Three parallel lists
pioneers = ["Ada Lovelace", "Alan Turing", "Grace Hopper", "Mary Jackson"]
facts = [
    "the first programmer of a theoretical computer",
    "the decoder of the German Enigma Code during World War 2",
    "the creator of the first compiler of a programming language",
    "the first Black female engineer at NASA",
     ]
images = ["AdaLovelace.png", "AlanTuring.png", "GraceHopper.png", "MaryJackson.png"]

print("Welcome to the Computer Science Pioneers Search App!")
print("Here are the pioneers in computer science:")
# TODO: Use a simple for-loop to traverse the pioneers list
#   and print out each name

person = input("Who do you want to learn about? ")

# TODO: Use a for-loop with i in the range of len(pioneers) to search
# In the loop, check if person in pioneers[i] (in will check substrings)
# If it is, print the facts[i] and do draw(images[i]) to display the image of the pioneer



</code>
<tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def findLine(self, lines, target):
        for line in lines:
            if "#" in line:
                index = line.find("#")
                line = line[0:index]
            if target in line.replace(" ", ""):
                #print(line)
                return line
        return "None"
    
    def test1(self):
        lines = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "").split("\n")
        count = self.getEditorText().count("for ")
        self.assertLessEqual(2, count, "Code should have two for-loops")
        self.assertIn("range", self.findLine(lines,"range(len(") , "Use range(len(pioneers)) to traverse the lists by index in the second for-loop")
        self.assertIn("pioneers[i]", self.findLine(lines,"pioneers[i]"), "Use pioneers[i] in the loop to check if the person is in the pioneers list")
        self.assertIn("facts[i]", self.findLine(lines,"facts[i]"), "Use facts[i] in the loop to print the fact")
        self.assertIn("draw(images[i])", self.findLine(lines,"draw(images[i])"), "Use draw(images[i]) in the loop to display the images")

myTests().main()
</tests>
</program>
</project>
        
      <p>The second part of the challenge is to create a quiz app about pioneers in computer science. The pioneers, 
        facts, and images are in parallel lists where the first pioneer in the pioneers list corresponds to the first fact in the facts
        list and the first image in the image list, and so on for each element in the lists.
      </p>

    <project label="coding-challenge-quiz-app">
      <title>Quiz App</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A quiz app uses 3 parallel lists to store the names of pioneers, facts about their contributions, and their images. 
          Use for loops to print out questions from the facts list and check the user's answers against the pioneers list.
        </p>
        <p>Extra challenges: Add and print a score variable. Do research to find more CS pioneers and add them to the lists. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python" add-files="AdaLovelace,AlanTuring,GraceHopper,MaryJackson">
        <preamble visible="no">
# Do not change the following draw function. It will display the image of the pioneer.
from image import *
def draw(image):
    img = Image(image)
    win = ImageWin(img.getWidth(), img.getHeight())
    img.draw(win)
          </preamble>      
        <code>
# Quiz App
# Three parallel lists
pioneers = ["Ada Lovelace", "Alan Turing", "Grace Hopper", "Mary Jackson"]
facts = [
    "the first programmer of a theoretical computer",
    "the decoder of the German Enigma Code during World War 2",
    "the creator of the first compiler of a programming language",
    "the first Black female engineer at NASA",
     ]
images = ["AdaLovelace.png", "AlanTuring.png", "GraceHopper.png", "MaryJackson.png"]

print("Welcome to the Computer Science Pioneers Quiz!")
print("Here are the pioneers in computer science:")

# TODO: Use a simple for-loop to traverse the pioneers list
#   and print out each name


# TODO: Use a for-loop with i in the range of len(pioneers) to present questions
# Create a question by adding "Who was" to each fact.
# Use draw(images[i]) to display the image of the pioneer
# Get the answer input from the user
# Check if the answer is correct by comparing it to the pioneers list
# Print Correct or Incorrect and the answer from the pioneers list


</code>
<tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui
class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def findLine(self, lines, target):
        for line in lines:
            if "#" in line:
                index = line.find("#")
                line = line[0:index]
            if target in line.replace(" ", ""):
                #print(line)
                return line
        return "None"
            
    def test1(self):
            lines = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "").split("\n")
            count = self.getEditorText().count("for ")
            self.assertLessEqual(2, count, "Code should have two for-loops")
            self.assertIn("range", self.findLine(lines,"range(len(") , "Use range(len(pioneers)) to traverse the lists by index in the second for-loop")
            self.assertIn("facts[i]", self.findLine(lines,"facts[i]"), "Use facts[i] in the loop to ask the question")
            self.assertIn("input(", self.findLine(lines,"input("), "Use input in the loop to ask the user for their answer")
            self.assertIn("else:", self.findLine(lines,"else:"), "Use if/else in the loop to check if the answer is correct")
            self.assertIn("pioneers[i]", self.findLine(lines,"pioneers[i]"), "Use pioneers[i] in the loop to check and print the answer") 
            self.assertIn("draw(images[i])", self.findLine(lines,"draw(images[i])"), "Use draw(images[i]) in the loop to display the images")

myTests().main()
</tests>
</program>
</project>

<p>The final part of this challenge is to create your own data lists and use it in a quiz app.
 Create 2 parallel lists with at least 3 elements each about something you are interested in, for example famous athletes and their teams, singers and their albums, actors and their movies, animals and their habitats, or any other topic you are interested in. If you'd like to do images, you can copy in the import and draw function from the previous quiz app and use complete http or https links to small images on the web. 
</p>

    <project label="coding-challenge-personal-quiz-app">
